---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Conditional from "../../components/Conditional.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import { builtInLabels } from "../../utils/built-in-labels"
import { componentsWithSlot } from "../../utils/components"

export const keywords = [
  "a11y",
  "aria",
  "focus ring",
  "forced colors",
  "high contrast",
  "keyboard navigation",
  "reduced motion",
  "screen reader",
]

const html = Astro.locals.framework === "html"
---

<Guide
  description="What the components give you for free, and what you still decide: names, order and content, tested with a keyboard and a screen reader."
  overline="Guide"
>
  <Fragment slot="title">Accessibility</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    A few basics to get you started. Open Props UI leans on native HTML, so a
    lot comes for free, but you still decide the names, the order and the
    content. Test with a keyboard and a screen reader.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="native-elements">
    <h2>Built on native elements</h2>
    <p>
      Components are styled native elements: <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>,
      <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, popovers and
      form inputs. The browser gives them their roles, keyboard support and
      focus handling, so almost no ARIA needs JavaScript. A few
      <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#javascript">scripts</DocLink> set it: a
      sortable <DocLink href="/components/table#sorting">Table</DocLink> sets
      <code>aria-sort</code> on the header you press, the
      <DocLink href="/components/text-field#password-toggle"
        >password toggle</DocLink
      >
      sets <code>aria-pressed</code>, a
      <DocLink href="/components/range#value">Range</DocLink> with a value
      suffix or labeled ticks sets <code>aria-valuetext</code>, and the
      character count, <DocLink href="/components/copy">Copy</DocLink> and
      <DocLink href="/components/toast">Toast</DocLink> announce through live
      regions.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/tabs">Tabs</DocLink> are radio buttons, and
        so is a <DocLink href="/components/toggle">Toggle</DocLink> group with
        single selection.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/dialog">Dialog</DocLink> and
        <DocLink href="/components/drawer">Drawer</DocLink> open with
        <code>showModal()</code> (or <code>command="show-modal"</code>), which
        makes the page behind them inert.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/accordion">Accordion</DocLink> is
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="accessible-names">
    <h2>Accessible names</h2>
    <p>Anything without visible text needs a name. The usual suspects:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Icon-only <DocLink href="/components/button#icon-only">Buttons</DocLink
        >:
        <Conditional>
          <Fragment slot="components"
            ><code>aria-label</code> (or <code>aria-labelledby</code
            >).</Fragment
          >
          <Fragment slot="html"><code>aria-label</code>.</Fragment>
        </Conditional>
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/checkbox">Checkbox</DocLink>,
        <DocLink href="/components/radio">Radio</DocLink> and
        <DocLink href="/components/switch">Switch</DocLink> without visible
        text:
        <Conditional>
          <Fragment slot="components"
            >keep the text and set <code>hideLabel</code>.</Fragment
          >
          <Fragment slot="html"
            >put the text in a <code>.ui-sr-only</code> element.</Fragment
          >
        </Conditional>
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/badge#accessibility">Badge</DocLink> counts
        need hidden context, so "5" is read as "5 unread messages".
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/avatar#accessibility">Avatar</DocLink> takes
        the person's name as <code>alt</code>, or <code>alt=""</code> when the
        name is shown next to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/carousel">Carousel</DocLink> needs a label.
        Its buttons and markers are named in CSS, with
        <code>--_button-previous-label</code>, <code>--_button-next-label</code>
        and <code>--_marker-label</code>, so translate those too, or the
        <code>previousLabel</code>, <code>nextLabel</code> and
        <code>markerLabel</code> props that set them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/tabs#accessibility">Tabs</DocLink> without
        panels get a name with <code>role="radiogroup"</code> and
        <code>aria-label</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="built-in-labels">
    <h2>Built-in labels</h2>
    <p>
      Some components write text of their own, in English unless you pass
      another. Translate each one for a page in another language:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">{html ? "Set" : "Prop"}</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {builtInLabels.map((label) => <tr>
          <td>
            <DocLink href={`/components/${label.page}`}
              >{label.component}</DocLink
            >
          </td>
          <td><code>{html ? label.html : label.prop}</code></td>
          <td>
            {(html ? label.shown : label.template) ?? label.default}
          </td>
        </tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    {!html && <p>
      <DocLink href="/components/rating#localization">Rating</DocLink> picks the
      plural form of <code>starLabel</code> for its <code>lang</code>, so pass
      <code>lang</code> to it too.
    </p>}
  </section>

  <section id="keyboard">
    <h2>Keyboard</h2>
    <p>
      Keyboard support is mostly what the native element does. Each component
      page lists its keys under Keyboard support. A quick tour:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/accordion#keyboard-support"
          >Accordion</DocLink
        >: <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> open and close it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/anchor#keyboard-support">Anchor</DocLink>
        and
        <DocLink href="/components/tooltip#keyboard-support">Tooltip</DocLink>:
        focus on the trigger shows them after a short delay, and <kbd>Esc</kbd>
        hides them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/carousel#keyboard-support">Carousel</DocLink
        >: arrow keys move between the markers and scroll to their items, in
        Chromium.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/dialog#keyboard-support">Dialog</DocLink>
        and <DocLink href="/components/drawer#keyboard-support">Drawer</DocLink
        >: <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves through the dialog. After the last element,
        focus goes to the browser UI, then back to the first element. The page
        behind is inert. <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it, unless you set
        <code>closedby="none"</code>, and focus goes back to the button that
        opened it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/menu#keyboard-support">Menu</DocLink>:
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves through the items and <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it.
        There are no arrow keys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/number-field#keyboard-support"
          >Number field</DocLink
        >: <kbd>Up Arrow</kbd> and <kbd>Down Arrow</kbd> in the input step the
        value.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/radio#keyboard-support">Radio</DocLink>:
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves to the checked radio, arrow keys move and select.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/range#keyboard-support">Range</DocLink>:
        arrow keys, <kbd>Page Up</kbd>, <kbd>Page Down</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd>
        and <kbd>End</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/select#keyboard-support">Select</DocLink>:
        the native select keys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/tabs#keyboard-support">Tabs</DocLink> and
        single-select
        <DocLink href="/components/toggle#keyboard-support"
          >Toggle groups</DocLink
        >: <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves into the group, arrow keys move and select.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/toast#keyboard-support">Toast</DocLink>:
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches a toast's buttons where the toaster sits in the
        page, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> dismisses the toast that has focus.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="focus">
    <h2>Focus</h2>
    <p>
      Every focusable element gets a <code>:focus-visible</code> ring from
      <code>normalize.css</code>. By default it's black on light surfaces and
      white on dark ones, so it shows up on any page background. Tune it with
      theme tokens:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more) {
  --ui-focus-ring-color: var(--ui-primary);
}

:where(html) {
  --ui-focus-ring-offset: 2px;
  --ui-focus-ring-style: solid;
  --ui-focus-ring-width: 2px;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      <code>--ui-focus-ring-inset</code> is used where the ring is drawn inside,
      like list items. With more contrast, the ring grows to 3px.
    </p>
    <p>
      Give a bright ring a dark edge with <code>--ui-focus-border-color</code>,
      and thicken it with <code>--ui-focus-border-width</code>:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more) {
  --ui-focus-border-color: oklch(0.153 0 0);
  --ui-focus-ring-color: oklch(0.899 0.186 97.9);
}

:where(html) {
  --ui-focus-border-width: 4px;
  --ui-focus-ring-offset: 0px;
  --ui-focus-ring-width: 3px;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Set <code>--ui-link-focus</code> and <code>--ui-button-focus</code> to
      <code>highlight</code> to fill focused links and buttons instead:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`:where(html) {
  --ui-button-focus: highlight;
  --ui-link-focus: highlight;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="contrast-and-motion">
    <h2>Contrast, forced colors and motion</h2>
    <p>
      These live on the <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> page:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/guide/theming#contrast">Contrast</DocLink>:
        <code>prefers-contrast: more</code> raises the contrast of text, borders
        and the focus ring. Force it with <code>.ui-contrast-more</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/guide/theming#forced-colors">Forced colors</DocLink>:
        components switch to system colors. Nothing to configure.
      </li>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/guide/theming#motion">Motion</DocLink>:
        <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> sets
        <code>--ui-motion: 0</code>. Override it with
        <code>.ui-motion-off</code> and <code>.ui-motion-on</code> on
        <code>html</code>, which also turn the page's smooth scrolling off and
        on.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="per-component">
    <h2>Per component</h2>
    <p>Some pages have their own notes:</p>
    <ul>
      {componentsWithSlot("accessibility").map(({ name, slug }) => <li>
        <DocLink href={`/components/${slug}#accessibility`}>{name}</DocLink>
      </li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="known-gaps">
    <h2>Known gaps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <DocLink href="/components/menu#keyboard-support">Menu</DocLink> has no
        arrow key navigation, only <kbd>Tab</kbd> and <kbd>Esc</kbd>. Keep that
        in mind for long menus. In Chromium, <kbd>Esc</kbd> in a submenu moves
        focus to the page instead of the submenu's item.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <DocLink href="/components/carousel#accessibility">Carousel</DocLink
        >'s buttons and markers are generated by CSS, and browsers still differ
        in how they name, focus and show them.
      </li>
      <li>
        A
        <DocLink href="/components/number-field#accessibility"
          >Number field</DocLink
        >
        step button changes the value without moving focus, so screen readers
        don't announce it. The arrow keys on the input do.
      </li>
      <li>
        Screen readers may read a
        <DocLink href="/components/rating#accessibility">Rating</DocLink> meter
        as a percentage, so put the score in the label.
      </li>
      <li>
        On touch, the links in an
        <DocLink href="/components/anchor#hover-trigger">Anchor</DocLink> hover
        card can't be reached: a tap on the trigger follows its link.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Not yet checked in every browser and screen reader:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Whether a busy button keeps its name and is announced as busy
        (<DocLink href="/components/spinner#buttons">Spinner</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Whether a tooltip's text is read as its trigger's description, and
        whether a long press shows it on touch
        (<DocLink href="/components/tooltip">Tooltip</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Whether Safari keeps the table roles of a
        <DocLink href="/components/table#in-a-card">Table</DocLink> that scrolls
        sideways and of a rich text table
        (<DocLink href="/components/typography#classless">Typography</DocLink>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Whether the typing indicator of a
        <DocLink href="/components/message">Message</DocLink> is announced when
        it appears.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Guide>
